{
  "rules": [
    "Regime bias: the model is too low during short bullish bursts (first ~5–15 days in Examples 1–3), then becomes too high when the market turns bearish (Examples 2–5). Apply a regime-dependent bias: add +1 to +2 €/t when recent momentum is positive; subtract −3 to −8 €/t when recent momentum turns negative.",
    "Turning-point lag: forecasts react slowly to reversals. When the last 3–5 days show a clear direction change, pull the whole path toward the new direction (increase slope in upturns; decrease slope in downturns) rather than keeping the prior trend.",
    "Downturn underestimation at longer horizons: beyond ~day 15–30, the model systematically fails to capture drawdowns and stays anchored near ~70–74 while realized prices fall to mid-60s/low-60s (Examples 2–5). Add an extra negative drift for horizons >15 days when recent volatility rises and momentum is negative.",
    "Spike handling: after upward spikes, the model mean-reverts too quickly and underpredicts the persistence of elevated levels (Examples 1–3 around the 72–73 area). Reduce mean-reversion strength for ~7–10 days after a spike (keep levels higher).",
    "Crash handling: after the market starts selling off, the model overshoots upward (predicts new highs) instead of reverting down (Examples 4–5). If price breaks below a recent support/short MA, cap forecast highs and enforce faster mean reversion downward.",
    "Level anchoring: forecasts cluster around ~70 regardless of realized trend (especially in Examples 2–5). Increase sensitivity to recent returns: widen forecast distribution and allow the level to migrate (down to mid-60s) when the last 10–15 days’ average is falling."
  ]
}
